## INTRODUCTION
Natural enzymes with high catalytic activity and substrate specificity have been broadly applied in biomedical, biochemistry, industrial catalysis, and environmental fields.1 Nevertheless, as biological macromolecules, the practical applications are gravely hindered by their intrinsic drawbacks, such as multifarious preparation/purification processes, high extraction cost, strict storage conditions, and particularly the low stability in severe environments.2 As promising alternatives to natural enzymes, nanozymes, defined as nanomaterials with inherent enzyme-mimetic properties, have aroused significant interest due to their large-scale preparation potential, low cost, convenient storage, and strong stability under harsh environments.3,4 So far, numerous nanomaterials, such as carbonbased nanomaterials (e.g., graphene, quantum dots, and carbon dots),5−7 noble-metal-based nanomaterials (e.g., Pd, Pt, Au, and $\mathbf{Ag}{\dot{}}$ ),8−10 and metal oxide-based nanomaterials (e.g., cobalt oxide, ferric oxide, manganese oxide, and ceria (CeO2)),9,11−13 have been demonstrated to possess specific enzyme-mimetic catalytic activities.
Especially, ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ nanomaterials have captured great attention due to their adjustable morphologies, profuse oxygen vacancies and structure defects, and easily reversible oxidation states of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ $\left(\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}/\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}\right)$ , which make them typical and prospective peroxidase mimics.11,14 However, compared with natural peroxidases, such as horseradish peroxidase (HRP), the peroxidase-like activity of bare $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ nanomaterial still remains to be improved.15 To date, diverse efforts have been devoted to ameliorate the peroxidase-like performance of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ -based
