nanomaterials, such as the adjustment of morphologies and crystal phases,14 the introduction of external environment stimuli (e.g., light),16 and the modification with other nanomaterials (e.g., noble metals and metal oxides).17,18 Among these strategies, loading noble metals on ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ surface to improve its catalytic performance is a promising strategy since introducing suitable heterogeneous atoms onto nanomaterials will commonly produce more surface defects.19 For example, the decoration of Pt nanoparticles on the surface of ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ nanorods $\left(\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\ \mathrm{NR}\right)$ can facilitate the peroxidase-like activity clearly, which is mainly ascribed to the strong interaction between $\mathrm{Pt}$ nanoparticles and $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NR}^{17}$ Indeed, some studies have been established in this subject, but it is still imperative to develop more appropriate and superior strategies for narrowing the gap between ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ -based nanozymes and natural peroxidases to satisfy the requirement of practical applications.
Recently, restricting active enzyme-like nanomaterials into the cavities of porous structures to enhance their catalytic activities has attracted considerable interest. The restricted structure not only prevents the active species from leaching but also augments the electron/mass transfer efficiency. Moreover, the increment of local amounts of active component within the restrictive cage will efficiently accelerate the catalytic process.2 .20 Up to present, multitudinous restriction carriers, such as carbon-based materials,21 silica-based materials,22 conducting polymers,20 and metal−organic frameworks (MOFs),23 have been exploited to improve the enzyme-mimetic activities. Among these restriction supports, MOFs, as a kind of characteristic porous polymers formed by self-assembly of organic linkers and metal ions through strong coordination bonds, demonstrate the advantages of mild synthetic conditions, high porosity, robust stability, large surface areas, and accessible active sites.24 Meanwhile, compared to other classical porous carriers, MOFs as restriction supports can offer exceptional stability against extreme pH conditions, high temperatures, organic solvents, and long-term storage.25 More importantly, MOFs are also regarded as a class of potential nanozymes to mimic natural peroxidases due to the mimicactive sites of polyvalent elements,26 which will play a synergistic effect with nanomaterials to improve the catalytic performance. From above, the combination of MOFs and $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ -based nanozymes will be beneficial to synergistically improve the catalytic performance of nanozymes through physical and chemical interaction.
In this work, a novel functionalized three-in-one peroxidase mimic with high catalytic activity and outstanding stability is constructed by restricting Au-loaded ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ NR $\left(1\%\mathrm{Au:CeO}_{2}\right.$ mass ratio) inside Cu-based MOFs $\mathrm{'Cu(PABA)},$ , termed $\mathrm{Au}_{1}/$ $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NR@Cu(PABA) (Scheme 1A). Multiple strategies, including morphology adjustment, surface modification, and nanomaterials restriction, are used to synergistically improve the performances of the artificial enzyme. Compared with different-shaped $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ nanocrystals, unrestricted ${\mathrm{Au}}/{\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ $\mathrm{NR,}$ and pure $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{PABA})}}$ , the $\mathbf{Au}_{1}/\mathbf{CeO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{NR}\ @\mathrm{Cu}$ (PABA) displays significantly enhanced activity in catalytic decomposition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ due to more oxygen defect sites existing on $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NR surface, synergistic effect among the individual constituents (Au, ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ $\mathrm{NR},$ and $\mathrm{Cu(PABA)}$ ), and the restricted structure of $\mathbf{Au}_{1}/\mathbf{CeO}_{2}$ NR inside $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{PABA})}}$ . The artificial enzyme can efficiently decompose $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to produce reactive oxygen species (ROS), including singlet oxygen $\left(^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\right)$ , hydroxyl radicals $(\bullet\mathrm{OH})$ , and superoxide anion radicals $\left(\bullet\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}\right)$ , that oxidize the colorless and nonfluorescent $\mid o\mid$ - phenylenediamine (OPD) to the yellow and fluorescent oxidation product 2,3-diaminophenazine (DAP) (right side of Scheme 1B). The steady-state kinetic analysis shows that the Michaelis constants $\left(K_{\mathrm{m}}\right)$ of $\mathbf{Au}_{1}/\mathbf{CeO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{NR}\ @\mathrm{Cu}$ (PABA) toward OPD and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ are both lower than those of HRP, indicating the stronger affinities of $\mathrm{Au_{1}}/\mathrm{CeO_{2}N R}\mathcal{Q}\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{PABA})$ to substrates. Meanwhile, the stability tests demonstrate that the ${\mathrm{Au}}_{1}/{\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ $\mathrm{{NR}}(\varpi\mathrm{{Cu}(P A B A)}$ possesses exceptional tolerance to long-term storage and extreme environments (strong acidic or basic media, high temperatures, and organic solvents). Moreover, the independent dual-response signals (absorbance and fluorescence intensity) from different transduction mechanisms can mutually support each other to improve the reliability of test results.27 In view of these superior properties, a colorimetric and fluorometric dual-mode sensing method based on $\mathbf{Au}_{1}/\mathbf{CeO}_{2}$ NR@Cu(PABA) for sensitive and selective detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is established. Also, a universal platform for quantification of biomarkers by combining the corresponding oxidase-catalyzed oxidation reaction is built (Scheme 1B). In this work, multiple biomarkers (glucose, galactose, and cholesterol) are selected as model target analytes, and their quantification in human serum or urine samples are all well-achieved, verifying the fabulous universality and the bright prospect of the platform in human healthcare. These study details also supply more thorough understandings and insights into the strategies for improving catalytic performance of enzyme-like nanomaterials, which will guide the engineering of nanozymes.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Chemicals and Materials. Chemicals and materials are detailed in the Supporting Information.
Instruments. Instruments are detailed in the Supporting Information.
Synthesis of ${\sf A u}_{1}/{\sf C e}0_{2}$ $\mathsf{N R@C u(P A B A)}$ . First, $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NR were prepared by the hydrothermal method with some modifications.28 Typically, $0.434\textrm{g}$ of cerium(III) nitrate hexahydrate $\left(\mathrm{Ce}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}\right.$ · $\left.6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right),$ and $4.80\mathrm{~g~}$ of sodium hydroxide $(\mathrm{NaOH})$ were separately dissolved in 2.5 and $17.5~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water, respectively. Then the $\mathrm{Ce}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}$ solution was added dropwise to the ${\mathrm{NaOH}}$ solution under stirring, followed by stirring at room temperature for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . The mixture was transferred into a polytetrafluoroethylene autoclave and hydrothermally treated at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . After that, the precipitate was centrifuged, washed with deionized water and ethanol several times, and then dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ to obtain the brightyellow $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NR.
Second, Au-loaded ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ NR with a $_\mathrm{Au:CeO}_{2}$ NR mass ratio of $1\%$ were prepared by the previously reported deposition−precipitation method via slight modifications.29 Briefly, $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NR $\left(60~\mathrm{mg}\right)$ were suspended in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water and sonicated for $20\ \mathrm{min},$ followed by the addition of $\mathrm{\DeltaNaOH}$ solution (0.5 M, $100\ \mu\mathrm{L},$ ). Then tetrachloroauric(III) acid $\mathrm{(HAuCl_{4})}$ ) solution $\left(100\mathrm{\mM},30\mu\mathrm{L}\right)$ and trisodium citrate solution $(50~\mathrm{mM},~140~\mu\mathrm{L})$ were successively added into the $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NR suspension under stirring. After continuous stirring for $^\mathrm{~1~h~}$ at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the precipitate was centrifugated, washed with deionized water several times, and then dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ to obtain the $\mathrm{Au}_{1}/\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NR. Similarly, other ${\mathrm{Au/CeO}}_{2}$ NR with different Au loading amounts were easily obtained by only altering the concentration of $\mathrm{HAuCl}_{4}$ solution and trisodium citrate solution; these are denoted as ${\cal A}{\sf u}_{n}/{\sf C e O}_{2}{\sf N R},$ where $\"{}\boldsymbol{n}\"\boldsymbol{}$ represents the mass ratio of Au to $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NR $(\%)$ . Meanwhile, the synthesis procedure of “Au” is the same as that of $\mathrm{{Au_{1}}/C e O_{2}}$ NR except without addition of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NR and $\mathrm{\DeltaNaOH}$ .
Finally, $\mathrm{Au}_{1}/\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NR were restricted inside Cu-based MOFs using the coprecipitation method as follows. Generally, $5.6~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{Au}_{1}\mathrm{\bar{/}C e}{\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ NR and $50\ \mathrm{\mg}$ of poly(vinylpyrrolidone) (PVP) were dispersed in $4~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water, stirred at room temperature for $\mathsf{6h},$ collected by centrifugation, and stored in $6~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water to obtain $\mathrm{{Au_{1}}/C e O_{2}}$ NR-PVP. Then cupric acetate solution $\mathrm{~\i00~mM}$ , $5~\mathrm{mL}$ ) and $p$ -aminobenzoic acid (PABA) dissolved in $50~\mathrm{mM}~\mathrm{NH_{4}A c}$ solution $\mathbf{\chi}^{\prime}12.5\ \mathrm{mM}$ , $10~\mathrm{mL}$ ) were successively added into the $\mathrm{Au}_{\mathrm{1}}/$ $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NR-PVP suspension, followed by stirring for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ at room temperature. The precipitate was centrifugated, washed with $\mathrm{\:HOAc/}$ $\mathrm{{NaOAc}}$ buffer solution $\left(100\mathrm{mM},\mathrm{pH}\mathrm{\bar{\}}5.0\right)$ and deionized water several times, and dried for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ to obtain the $\mathrm{Au_{1}/C e O_{2}}$ $\mathrm{NR}@$ $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{PABA})}}$ composite. Additionally, the synthesis procedure of pure $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{PABA})}}$ is the same as that of $\mathrm{Au_{1}/C e O_{2}N R}_{@\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{PABA})$ except without addition of ${\mathrm{Au}}_{1}/{\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ NR and PVP. Notably, PVP is used as a surfactant to adsorb on the surface of ${\mathrm{Au}}_{1}/{\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ NR by the coordination interactions between $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C}}={\mathrm{O}}$ in the pyrrolidone ring of PVP and Au or Ce in $\mathrm{Au_{1}/C e O_{2}\ N R}$ and stabilize $\mathrm{Au_{1}/C e O_{2}\ N\bar{R}}$ in the reaction solution. Additionally, it possesses an excellent affinity to $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{PABA})}}$ through coordination interaction between $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C}}={\mathrm{O}}$ groups in PVP and copper ions in $\mathtt{C u}$ -based MOFs30 and hydrogen-bonding interactions between $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C}}={\mathrm{O}}$ groups of PVP and amino/carboxyl groups of PABA.31
Peroxidase-like Activity. The peroxidase-like activity of various enzyme mimics $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ nanooctahedra $(\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NO})$ , $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ nanocubes $(\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\ \mathrm{NC})$ , ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ NR, ${\mathrm{Au}}_{n}/{\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ $\mathrm{NR},$ $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{PABA})}}$ , and $\mathbf{Au}_{1}/\mathbf{CeO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{{NR}}(\varpi\mathrm{{Cu}(P A B A))}$ were studied by catalyzing $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to oxidize the classical substrate OPD. Typically, $10~\mu\mathrm{L}$ of peroxidase mimic dispersion, $50\mu\mathrm{L}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ solution, and $50\mu\mathrm{L}$ of OPD solution were added into phosphate buffer solution (0.1 M, $\mathrm{pH}~7.0\$ ) with a final volume of $500~\mu\mathrm{L}$ . The final concentrations of peroxidase mimic, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ and OPD are $20~\mu\mathrm{g/mL},~200~\mu\mathrm{M},$ and $60~\mu\mathrm{M},$ , respectively. After being incubated at $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $40~\mathrm{min}$ , the fluorescence intensity at $562~\mathrm{nm}$ was measured using a fluorescence spectrophotometer $\left(\lambda_{\mathrm{ex}}\right.$ $\l=411\ \mathrm{nm}$ ). In addition, the group without $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was set as negative control, and the maximum fluorescence intensity value of the peroxidase mimic−OPD catalytic system with the fluorescence intensity value of the blank group removed was set as $100\%$ relative activity.
Detection of ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2},$ Glucose, Galactose, and Cholesterol. For $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ detection, $10\mu\mathrm{L}$ of $\mathrm{Au_{1}/C e O_{2}N R}_{@\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{PABA})$ , $50\mu\mathrm{L}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ standard solutions with different concentrations, and $50~\mu\mathrm{L}$ of OPD solution were added into phosphate buffer solution $\left(0.1\ \mathrm{M},\ \mathrm{pH}\ 7.0\right)$ ) with a final volume of $500~\mu\mathrm{L}$ . The final concentrations of ${\bf A u}_{1}/{\bf C e}{\bf O}_{2}$ $\mathrm{{NR}}(\varpi\mathrm{{Cu}(P A B A)}$ and OPD are $20~\mu\mathrm{g/mL}$ and $60~\mu\mathrm{M},$ respectively. After incubation at $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $40~\mathrm{min}$ , the absorbance at $417~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and the fluorescence intensity at $562\mathrm{nm}$ $\mathit{\lambda}_{\mathrm{{k}\mathrm{{x}}}}^{\prime}=411\mathrm{{nm}},$ were measured at each $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration using a UV−vis absorption spectrophotometer and a fluorescence spectrophotometer, respectively.
Glucose, galactose, or cholesterol quantification was performed through the detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ produced by the corresponding oxidase (glucose oxidase $\mathbf{\tilde{G}l u}\ \mathbf{Ox})$ , galactose oxidase (Gal $\left|\operatorname{Ox}\right\rangle$ ), or cholesterol oxidase (Cho $\mathrm{O}\mathbf{x},$ ))-catalyzed oxidation reaction. Briefly, $10\mu\mathrm{L}$ of the glucose, galactose, or cholesterol standard solution with a given concentration was first incubated with $40\mu\mathrm{L}$ of Glu $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{Ox},}\end{array}$ Gal $\begin{array}{r}{{{\mathrm{Ox}}},}\end{array}$ or Cho $\mathrm{O}\mathbf{x}$ at $40\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $20\ \mathrm{\min}$ to produce $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . The final concentrations of oxidases are all $20\:\:\mu\mathrm{g/mL}$ . Then the next procedures were followed as in the above $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ assay. Additionally, see the Supporting Information for detailed operating procedures of glucose, galactose, and cholesterol detection in human serum or urine samples.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Characterization of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ Nanocrystals. $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ nanocrystals with octahedron, cube, and rod shapes were prepared by the hydrothermal methods (Figure S1). Their morphological structures and optical properties were investigated by transmission electron microscopy (TEM) (Figure 1A−C) and UV−vis absorption spectrophotometry (Figure 1D), respectively. The related results are given in the Supporting Information.
To gain insight into the oxygen defect sites on the surfaces of three ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ nanocrystals, high-resolution TEM (HRTEM), electron spin resonance (ESR) spectroscopy, Raman spectroscopy, and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analyses were implemented. In each HRTEM image of three ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ nanocrystals (Figure S2), slight lattice disorder and dislocation can be locally observed, reflecting the existence of oxygen defects on the surface of ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ NO, $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NC, and ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ NR.32,33 In ESR test, each ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ nanocrystal has a symmetrical peak at the $g\ =\ 2.003$ (Figure 1E), which is attributed to unpaired electrons in the oxygen defect sites of nanomaterials, and the signal intensity can represent the relative amount of oxygen defect sites.34 Among the three $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ nanocrystals, $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NR exhibit the strongest signal, implying that they have the most oxygen defect sites. Notably, the relative amounts of oxygen defect sites for three $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ nanocrystals generally can also be reflected by the ratio of the Raman peak intensity at ${\sim}595~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ (corresponding to the defect-induced (D) mode) to that at $\mathrm{{\sim}461~c m^{\hat{-}1}}$ (belonging to the vibration $\left(\mathrm{F}_{2\mathrm{g}}\right)$ mode of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ fluorite phase).35 Figure 1F,G shows that the $\smile I_{595}/I_{461}$ ratios for three ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ nanocrystals rise with the order of ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ NO (0.016), ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ NC (0.021), and $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NR (0.175), further revealing ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ NR possess more oxygen defect sites than $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NO and $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NC. In Figure $\mathrm{1H},$ the highresolution XPS spectra of Ce 3d can be divided into eight peaks, labeled as $\nu^{\prime}$ and $u^{\prime}$ for $\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}$ and $\nu,\nu^{\prime\prime},\nu^{\prime\prime\prime},u,u^{\prime\prime}$ , and $\overset{\cdot\dag\prime}{u}$ for $\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}$ . The content of $\mathsf{C e}^{3+}$ can be utilized to infer the amount of the oxygen defect sites,36 and the peak area ratio of two valence states for Ce element $(\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}/\mathrm{Ce}^{4+})$ ranks as follows: $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NR $(0.43)\ >\ \mathrm{CeO}_{2}\ \mathrm{NC}\ (0.22)\ >\ \mathrm{CeO}_{2}\ \mathrm{NO}$ (0.11) (Figure 1I). XPS spectra of $\mathrm{~o~}$ 1s are fitted into two bands at ${\sim}531.0$ and ${\sim}529.3\ \mathrm{eV},$ assigned to surface oxygen defect sites $(\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{sur}})$ and lattice oxygen $\left(\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{lat}}\right)$ , respectively. Similarly, the $\mathrm{{O}_{\mathrm{{sur}}}/\mathrm{{O}_{\mathrm{{lat}}}}}$ ratio has a sequence of ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ NR $\left(1.70\right)\ >\ \mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NC $(0.96)>\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NO (0.36) (Figure S3). These XPS data confirm that ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ NR have the most oxygen defect sites compared with $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NO}$ and $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NC},$ which is in agreement with the results of ESR spectroscopy and Raman spectroscopy analyses.
To verify that the peroxidase-like activity of three $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ nanocrystals is positively correlated upon their surface amounts of oxygen defect sites, their respective catalytic activities were conducted through catalyzing $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to oxidize OPD (Figure 1J). Note that OPD is a classic peroxidase probe, which can be oxidized by ROS produced through the enzymatic decomposition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . The as-produced oxidation product (DAP) is yellow, showing maximum absorption peak at $417~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and fluorescence emission band at $562\ \mathrm{nm}$ $\begin{array}{r}{\bar{(\lambda)}_{\mathrm{ex}}=411\ \mathrm{nm}.}\end{array}$ ). As depicted in Figure ${\mathrm{1K}},$ three $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ nanocrystals all have peroxidase-like activity, and particularly, the ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ NR show a more exceptional catalytic performance than the others. Meanwhile, kinetic analysis for each $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ nanocrystal was conducted to acquire the $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ and maximal reaction velocity $\left(V_{\mathrm{max}}\right)$ (Figures 1L, S4, and S5). As listed in Table S1, $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NR display the lowest $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ and the highest $V_{\mathrm{max}}$ to both $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and OPD, which suggest the best affinity toward substrates and the highest reaction rate of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\ \mathrm{NR},$ followed by $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NC and $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NO. The results of relative activity analysis and kinetic analysis are well-consistent with the aforementioned trend of oxygen defect site amounts on the surface of three $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ nanocrystals. All these experimental data demonstrate that the peroxidase-like activity of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ nanocrystals is oxygen-defect-dependent. In a word, the relatively larger amount of oxygen defect sites on $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NR surface can promote a higher mobility of the bulk oxygen species, provide more active sites for transferring electrons, and further facilitate the decomposition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to generate more ROS.34,37−39 Therefore, $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NR present the highest peroxidase-like catalytic activity in contrast with ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ NC and $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NO.
Characterization of ${\sf A u}_{n}/{\sf C e}0_{2}$ NR. Au was loaded onto ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ NR surface through the facile deposition−precipitation method (Figure S6). The morphological structure and lattice fringe of $\mathbf{Au}_{1}/\mathbf{CeO}_{2}$ NR were investigated by TEM and HRTEM (Figure $^{2\mathrm{A,B}}$ ). The detailed data are given in the Supporting Information. Additionally, Figure 2C shows the Xray diffraction (XRD) patterns of ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ NR and $\mathbf{Au}_{1}/\mathbf{CeO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{NR,}$ both exhibiting strong diffraction peaks at $28.55^{\circ}$ , $33.08^{\circ}$ , and $47.48^{\circ}$ , attributed to (111), (200), and (220) crystal facets of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ with cubic fluorite structure (JCPDS no. 43-1002, space group $F m3m$ ). Obviously, the crystalline form of ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ NR remains unchanged after deposition of Au. These data indicate that loading of gold component has no effect on either morphology or crystalline structure of ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ NR. Meanwhile, no significant diffraction peaks of Au are observed, ascribed to its low loading and weak crystallization.40,41 Moreover, the elemental mapping analysis depicted in Figure $2\mathrm{D}{-}\mathrm{G}$ is also indicative of the even distribution of Ce, O, and Au elements throughout $\mathrm{{Au_{1}}/C e O_{2}}$ NR. Figure 2H shows that the zeta potential of $\mathrm{\bfAu}_{1}/\mathrm{\bfCe}{\bf O}_{2}$ NR $\:(15.83\ \mathrm{mV})\:$ is lower than that of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NR $\left(28.63~\mathrm{mV}\right)$ , attributed to the deposition of negative zeta potential of Au species $\left(-19.23~\mathrm{mV}\right)$ . The above results suggest that Au is successfully homogeneously loaded on $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NR surface.
Given the assumption that loading of Au on $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NR surfaces would enhance their peroxidase-like activity, the catalytic performances of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NR and ${\mathrm{Au}}_{n}/{\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ NR were studied and compared (Figure 2I). As displayed in Figure 2J, the four ${\mathrm{Au}}_{n}/{\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ NR nanocrystals prepared with different mass ratio of Au to ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ NR all exhibit superior peroxidaselike activity over pure $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\ \mathrm{NR},$ verifying above hypothesis. The improved catalytic performance could be attributable to the synergistic effect and efficient electron transfer between Au and $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NR, which leads to the alteration of electronic structure of heterostructures, augmenting the oxidation rate of OPD with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ .42 Besides, the peroxidase-like activity of $\mathbf{Au}_{n}/$ $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NR first increases until the mass ratio of Au to ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}{\mathrm{NR}}$ reaches $1\%$ (namely, $\mathrm{Au}_{1}/\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NR})$ and then decreases when the mass ratio of Au to $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NR increases to $2\%$ and $3\%$ (namely, $\mathrm{Au}_{2}/\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NR and $\mathrm{Au}_{3}/\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NR})$ , which is caused by Au aggregation (Figure S7)-induced reduction of active sites on $_\mathrm{Au\mathrm{-}C e O}_{2}$ contact boundary and decrease of the adsorption ability for reaction substrates.43,44 Hence, $\mathrm{Au}_{1}/$ $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NR were selected for further experiments.
Characterization of ${\sf A u}_{1}/{\sf C e}0_{2}$ $\mathsf{N R@C u(P A B A)}$ . $\mathrm{Au}_{1}/\$ ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ NR were restricted inside $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{PABA})}}$ , which is composed of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ linked with the PABA ligand (Scheme 1A), to achieve further enhancement of peroxidase-like activity.
Notably, the PABA is used as a multidentate organic ligand containing amino and carboxyl groups, which would selfassemble with copper ions to form coordination polymers, namely, $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{PAB}\bar{A})}}$ . The morphologies of ${\mathrm{Au}}_{1}/{\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ $\mathrm{NR}@$ $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{PABA})}}$ and $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{PABA})}}$ were characterized by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and TEM (Figure 3A−D). The $\mathbf{Au}_{1}/\mathbf{CeO}_{2}$ NR@Cu(PABA) clearly shows an ellipsoid structure with the length of $6{\mathrm{-}}10\ \mu\mathrm{m}_{\cdot}$ , which is similar to that of $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{PABA})}}$ . From the XRD patterns of ${\bf A u}_{1}/{\bf C e}{\bf O}_{2}$ $\mathrm{{NR}}(\varpi\mathrm{{Cu}(P A B A)}$ and $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{PABA})}}$ (Figure 3E), the positions of all diffraction peaks in $\mathrm{Au_{1}/C e O_{2}N R}@\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{PABA})$ match well with those in $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{PABA})}}$ , demonstrating the same stable main crystalline structure between $\mathrm{Au_{1}/C e O_{2}\ N R}\ @{\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{PABA})}$ and $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{PABA})}}$ . These results suggest that the structural integrity of $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{PABA})}}$ can be retained when $\mathrm{Au}_{1}/\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NR are present. Meanwhile, ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption experiment is executed to investigate the surface characteristics of ${\mathrm{Au}_{1}}/{\mathrm{CeO}_{2}}$ $\mathrm{{NR}}(\varpi\mathrm{{Cu}(P A B A)}$ . Figure 3F shows that the typical ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption−desorption isotherms are recognized as type $\mathrm{IV_{\it\perp}}$ , indicating a mesoporous structure in the ${\bf A u}_{1}/{\bf C e}{\bf O}_{2}$ $\mathrm{NR}@$ $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{PAB}\bar{A})}}$ composites.45 The specific surface area, pore volume, and average pore size of ${\bf A u}_{1}/{\bf C e}{\bf O}_{2}$ $\mathsf{N R}@\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{P A B A})$ are calculated to be $\bar{1}5.2\mathrm{~m}^{2}/\mathrm{g},0.0654\mathrm{~cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g},$ and $19.6\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively (Figure 3F,G). These suggest that the $\mathrm{{Au_{1}}/C e O_{2}}$ $\mathrm{{NR}}(\varpi\mathrm{{Cu}(P A B A)}$ composite has a large specific surface area and a good porosity. The energy-dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) spectrum of $\mathrm{A}\mathrm{\bar{u}}_{1}/\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NR}\bar{@}\mathrm{Cu}(\bar{\mathrm{PABA}})$ reveals that its main elemental components contain not only the same C, O, N, and Cu elements as $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{PABA})}}$ but also Ce and Au elements, confirming the presence of $\mathbf{Au}_{1}/\mathbf{CeO}_{2}$ NR (Figure S8). The corresponding elemental mappings show that the $\mathbf{C},$ O, N, Cu, Ce, and Au elements were evenly distributed throughout $\mathrm{Au}_{1}/\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{{NR}}(\varpi\mathrm{{Cu}(P A B A)}$ (Figure $3\mathrm{H-O})$ . Additionally, Figure S9 displays that the zeta potential of $\mathrm{Au_{1}/C e O_{2}\ N R}\mathcal{O}\mathrm{Cu(PABA)}$ is $26.63\ \mathrm{eV},$ , which is lower than the sum of the zeta potentials of pure $\mathbf{Au}_{1}/\mathbf{CeO}_{2}$ NR (15.83 $\mathrm{eV},$ and blank $\mathrm{{Cu}(\bar{P}A B A)}$ $\left(16.8\bar{3}\mathrm{~\eV}\right)$ , revealing that $\mathrm{Au}_{1}/$ $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NR and $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{PABA})}}$ are not simply combined in a physical way, which still needs further exploration. All these findings express the successful synthesis of ${\bf A u}_{1}/{\bf C e}{\bf O}_{2}$ $\mathrm{NR}@$ $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{P}\bar{\mathrm{{ABA}}})}}$ .
Enhanced Peroxidase-like Activity of ${\sf A u}_{1}/{\sf C e}0_{2}$ $N R@$ ${\mathsf{C u}}({\mathsf{P A B A}})$ . The catalytic performance of $\mathrm{{Au_{1}}/C e O_{2}}$ $\mathrm{NR}@$ $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{PABA})}}$ as a peroxidase mimic was first investigated (right side of Scheme 1B). As shown in Figure 4A, there are no obvious fluorescence intensity changes in the $\mathrm{Au}_{1}/\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NR}\varpi$ $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{PABA})}}$ , OPD, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ $\mathrm{O}_{2},\mathrm{Au}_{1}/\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NR}@\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{PABA}){-}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{\OPD{-}H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ reaction systems. Meanwhile, a weak fluorescence emission is observed in the $\mathrm{Au}_{1}/\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{NR}@$ $\mathrm{Cu(PABA){\mathrm{-}}O P D}$ catalytic system, suggesting a low oxidaselike property of $\Delta\mathrm{u}_{1}/C e O_{2}~\mathrm{NR}\ @\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{PABA})$ , which can be easily deducted through setting blank groups (see the Experimental Section). Significantly, in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ $\mathrm{Au}_{1}/\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{{NR}}@\mathrm{{Cu}}(\mathrm{{PABA}})$ can effectively oxidize OPD to produce DAP, giving rise to a strong fluorescence emission band at $562\ \mathrm{nm}$ . These results confirm that $\mathrm{Au}_{1}/$ $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NR}_{@}\mathrm{Cu}\big(\mathrm{PABA}\big)$ can serve as an outstanding peroxidase mimic.
To investigate the enhanced catalytic performance of $\mathrm{Au}_{1}/$ ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ $\mathrm{{NR}}(\partial\mathrm{{Cu}(P A B A)}$ , its peroxidase-like activity was compared to those of unrestricted $\mathbf{Au}_{1}/\mathbf{CeO}_{2}$ NR and pure $\mathrm{{Cu}(\bar{P}A B A)}$ . Figure 4B clearly shows that the peroxidase-like activity of $\mathrm{Au_{1}/C e O_{2}N R}_{@\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{PABA})$ is separately ${\sim}2.41$ and ${\sim}1.49$ times higher than those of $\mathbf{Au}_{1}/\mathbf{CeO}_{2}$ NR and $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{PABA})}}$ , respectively, demonstrating the prominently improved performance of $\mathbf{Au}_{1}/\mathbf{CeO}_{2}$ NR@Cu(PABA) in catalytic decomposition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ which might be ascribed to the synergistic effect between $\mathrm{Au}_{1}/\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NR and $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{PABA})}}$ and the restricted structure of ${\mathrm{Au}}_{1}/{\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ NR inside Cu(PABA). Notably, the unique restricted structure can increase the local amounts of active component, facilitate electron/mass transport property, and provide more active sites for catalysis.20,46
Catalytic Mechanism of $\mathsf{A u}_{1}/\mathsf{C e O}_{2}\mathsf{N R@C u(P A B A)}$ . To deeply comprehend the catalytic mechanism of $\mathrm{Au}_{1}/\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{{NR}}(\varpi\mathrm{{Cu}(P A B A)}$ as a peroxidase mimic, the radical scavenging test was first performed. Three ROS scavengers, including sodium azide $(\mathsf{N a N}_{3})$ , isopropyl alcohol (IPA), and $p{\cdot}$ - benzoquinone (PBQ) were used to capture $^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2},\cdot_{\mathrm{OH}}$ , and $\cdot_{\mathrm{O}_{2}}-$ , respectively. As illustrated in Figure 4C, the introduction of any one of three scavengers causes a decrease in the relative catalytic activity compared with that of blank, and the PBQinduced decrease is the most. The results indicate the crucial role of $\cdot_{\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}}$ and the auxiliary roles of ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and •OH. Further, ESR test was also conducted to confirm the production of ROS in the ${\bf A u}_{1}/{\bf C e}{\bf O}_{2}$ ${\mathrm{NR}}@{\mathrm{Cu}}({\mathrm{PABA}})-{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ system. 2,2,6,6- Tetramethylpiperidine (TEMP) and $^{5,5}$ -dimethyl-1-pyrroline $N$ -oxide (DMPO) were selected as spin-trapping agents to capture ROS $({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2},{}^{\cdot}\mathrm{OH},$ and $\cdot_{\mathrm{O}_{2}}-\cdot$ . As shown in Figure $\ensuremath{4\mathrm{D}}-$ F, there are no obvious signal peaks when ${\bf A u}_{1}/{\bf C e}{\bf O}_{2}$ $\mathrm{NR}@$ $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{PABA})}}$ or $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is present alone; conversely, strong characteristic signals of $\mathrm{\dot{TE}M P-}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (1:1:1), DMPO−•OH (1:2:2:1), and $\mathrm{DMPO}{-}^{\bullet}\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}$ (1:1:1:1) can be obtained when $\mathrm{Au_{1}}/\mathrm{CeO_{2}}\mathrm{NR}\varpi\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{PABA})$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ coexist. These findings verify that $\mathrm{Au_{1}}/\mathrm{CeO_{2}}\mathrm{NR}\varpi\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{PABA})$ can efficiently catalyze the decomposition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to generate multiple ROS $^{\mathrm{{1}}}\mathrm{{O}}_{2},$ $\cdot_{\mathrm{OH}}$ , and $\cdot_{\mathrm{O}_{2}}-\cdot$ ), thereby exhibiting a perfect peroxidase-like catalytic activity.
Steady-State Kinetic Analysis. Kinetic analysis was conducted by altering the concentration of one substrate $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\right)$ and remaining the other (OPD) unchanged, and vice versa. Figure 5A,B shows the curves of the initial reaction velocity versus the concentration of substrate $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\right)$ or OPD), which follow the characteristic Michaelis−Menten equation (eq S1) well. Then the curves were converted to corresponding Lineweaver−Burk double-reciprocal plots (eq S2) to evaluate the kinetic parameters, including $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ and $V_{\mathrm{max}}$ (Figure 5C,D; see detailed calculation process in the Supporting Information). The calculated kinetic parameters ( $\mathrm{\Delta}\cdot\mathrm{\Delta}K_{\mathrm{m}}$ and $V_{\mathrm{max}})$ of $\mathrm{Au}_{1}/\$ $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NR}_{@}\mathrm{Cu}\big(\mathrm{PABA}\big)$ were compared with those of HRP and other peroxidase mimics. As exhibited in Table S2, the $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ value of $\mathrm{Au_{1}}/\mathrm{CeO_{2}N R}\mathcal{Q}\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{PABA})$ for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ $\left(0.0414\mathrm{mM}\right)$ is 8.21-fold lower than that of HRP $\left(0.34~\mathrm{mM}\right)$ and is also lower than or analogous to those of other peroxidase mimics $(0.29-$ $450~\mathrm{~mM},$ ). Meanwhile, ${\bf A u}_{1}/{\bf C e}{\bf O}_{2}$ $\mathrm{{NR}}_{(\ell)\mathrm{{Cu}}}(\mathrm{{PABA})}$ also exhibits lower or similar $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ value $\left(0.1887\ \mathrm{\mM}\right)$ toward OPD in comparison with HRP $\left(0.59~\mathrm{\mM}\right)$ and other peroxidase mimics $\mathrm{^{\prime}0.068{-}1.212\ m M}\mathrm{})$ . As is widely known, $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ value reflects the enzyme affinity toward the substrate, and a lower $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ value describes a stronger binding affinity between enzyme and substrate.47 The data reveal the strong affinities of $\mathrm{Au_{1}/C e O_{2}\ N R}\ @\mathrm{Cu(PABA)}$ to both $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and OPD (due to the large specific surface area and highly porous structure in $\mathrm{Au_{1}}/\mathrm{CeO_{2}N R}\mathcal{Q}\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{PABA})$ , which can help provide abundant active sites and high electron conductivity $^{48-50}$ ) and an ultrasensitive analysis potential in biomedical applications.
Figure $^{\mathrm{5E,F}}$ display the Lineweaver−Burk double reciprocal plots of ${\bf A u}_{1}/{\bf C e}{\bf O}_{2}$ $\mathrm{{NR}}(\varpi\mathrm{{Cu}(P A B A)}$ with varied $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (or OPD) amounts in the presence of different fixed OPD (or $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\overline{{\cdot}}$ ) concentrations. Each panel shows typical parallel lines, suggesting the characteristics of a ping-pong-like mechanism. The above results confirm that $\mathbf{Au}_{1}/\mathbf{CeO}_{2}$ NR@Cu(PABA)
reacts with the first substrate, releases the corresponding products, and then reacts with the second substrate.
Stability of ${\sf A u}_{1}/{\sf C e}0_{2}$ $\mathsf{N R@C u(P A B A)}$ . Normally, exposing functional substances to severe environments, such as strong acidic or basic media, high temperatures, and organic solvents, will lead to the reduction or even loss of activity. Hence, the stability of $\mathrm{{Au_{1}}/C e O_{2}}$ $\mathrm{{NR}}(\varpi\mathrm{{Cu}(P A B A)}$ under distinct severe environments was measured and compared with that of the natural HRP. In Figure $\mathrm{6A},$ the $\mathrm{Au}_{1}/\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{NR}({\widehat{\alpha}})$ $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{PABA})}}$ displays a similar peroxidase-like activity (above $85\%$ ) after being pretreated under different $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ values ranging from 4 to 12 for $120~\mathrm{min}$ . Differently, the catalytic activity of HRP presents a trend of increase first and decrease later in the $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ range of 4−12. Notably, HRP only reserves $2.12\%$ and $34.9\%$ of its original catalytic activity after pretreatment at $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ 4 and 12 for $120\ \mathrm{min}$ , which are much less than $96.8\%$ and $100\%$ of original activity of $\mathrm{Au_{1}/C e O_{2}N R}_{@\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{PABA})$ at the same $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ , respectively. Next, the thermal stabilities of $\mathbf{Au}_{1}/$ $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NR@Cu(PABA) and HRP were investigated and compared. Figure 6B illustrates that the ${\bf A u}_{1}/{\bf C e}{\bf O}_{2}$ $\mathrm{NR}@$ $\mathrm{{Cu}(\bar{P}A B A)}$ almost does not deactivate after incubation at 30, 40, 50, 60, or even $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $120~\mathrm{{min}}$ (with relative activities above $87\%$ ); however, HRP loses approximately $85\%$ of its initial activity after incubation at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and totally loses its activity when the temperature is higher than $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . In addition, $\mathrm{Au_{1}}/\mathrm{CeO_{2}N R}\mathcal{Q}\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{PABA})$ and HRP were placed in various organic solvents, including $N,N.$ -dimethylformamide (DMF), acetone (AC), ethyl acetate (EA), dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO), methanol $\left({\mathrm{MeOH}}\right)$ , and ethylene glycol (EG) for $120\ \mathrm{\min}$ to examine their respective tolerances to organic solvents. Figure 6C shows that the $\mathbf{Au}_{1}/\mathbf{CeO}_{2}$ $\operatorname{NR}(\varnothing\operatorname{Cu-}$ (PABA) can maintain more than $62\%$ of its initial catalytic activity after being placed in the above organic solvents for 120 min; conversely, the HRP only exhibits $1-44\%$ of its original catalytic activity under treatment with the above solvents. These results indicate that $\mathbf{Au}_{1}/\mathbf{CeO}_{2}$ NR@Cu(PABA) possesses outstanding $\mathsf{p H}$ , thermal, and organic solvent stability compared with natural HRP.
Furthermore, the storage stability of ${\bf A u}_{1}/{\bf C e}{\bf O}_{2}$ $\mathrm{NR}@$ $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{PABA})}}$ was tested and compared with that of HRP. The $\mathbf{Au}_{1}/\mathbf{CeO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{{NR}}(\varpi\mathrm{{Cu}(P A B A)}$ and HRP stock solutions were stored in a $4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ refrigerator, and the corresponding catalytic activities were detected every 5 days. Though the activity of $\mathrm{Au_{1}}/\mathrm{CeO_{2}N R}\mathcal{Q}\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{PABA})$ drops with time, it still holds $94\%$ of the original activity 1 month later, which is higher than the $78\%$ of original activity of HRP (Figure 6D), demonstrating a superior long-term storage stability of $\mathrm{Au}_{1}/\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{NR}@$ $\mathrm{{C}\bar{u}(\mathrm{{PABA})}}$ .
Measurement of ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2},$ Glucose, Galactose, and Cholesterol. Prior to measurement, the following parameters were first optimized: (a) OPD concentration, (b) ${\mathrm{Au}}_{1}/{\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ $\mathrm{{NR}}(\varpi\mathrm{{Cu}(P A B A)}$ amount, (c) solution $\mathrm{\pH_{\mathrm{\ell}}}$ (d) incubation temperature, and (e) response time. Respective data are shown in Figures 7 and S10. The following experimental conditions were found to give the best results: (a) $60\mu\mathrm{M};$ (b) $20\mu\mathrm{g/mL};$ (c) 7.0; (d) $40\ {}^{\circ}\mathrm{C};$ and (e) $40~\mathrm{min}$ .
Under above-obtained optimum experimental conditions, a $\mathbf{Au}_{1}/\mathbf{CeO}_{2}$ $\mathbf{NR}@\mathbf{Cu}$ (PABA)-OPD sensing platform was constructed for colorimetric and fluorometric detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Figure S11A displays the UV−vis absorption spectra of $\mathrm{\bfAu}_{1}/\mathrm{\bfCe}{\bf O}_{2}$ NR@Cu(PABA)−OPD system at different concentrations of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ and its upper inset shows the corresponding naked-eye images of different reaction solutions. The absorbance at $417~\mathrm{nm}$ increases gradually along with the increment of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration from 0.05 to $200~\mu\mathrm{M}$ (Figure S11B), which is linear when the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration ranged between 0.05 and $20~\mu\mathbf{M}$ . The linear regression equation is $y=0.00256x+0.0610$ $\begin{array}{r}{{\bf\nabla}^{'}R^{2}=0.9979,}\end{array}$ , and the limit of detection (LOD) is calculated to be $0.083~\mu\mathrm{M}~\left(3\sigma/k\right)$ (Figure S11C). Meanwhile, the fluorometric mode also displays an excellent linearity in the concentration range $0.05{-}20~\mu\mathrm{M}$ $(y~=~84.3x~+~1522$ , $R^{2}~=~0.9975)$ , and the calculated LOD is $0.077\ \mu\mathrm{M}$ (Figure $S11\mathrm{D-F},$ ).
Additionally, diverse possible interference substances involving biomacromolecules (Glu $\begin{array}{r}{|\mathrm{O}\mathbf{x},}\end{array}$ Gal $\begin{array}{r}{{{\mathrm{Ox}}},}\end{array}$ Cho $\begin{array}{r}{{{\mathrm{Ox}}},}\end{array}$ bovine serum albumin (BSA), and human serum albumin (HSA)), inorganic ions $(\mathrm{Na^{+}},\mathrm{K^{+}},{\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}},\mathrm{Mg^{2+}},$ and $\mathrm{PO_{4}}^{3-}\mathrm{\overline{{{\right)}}}},$ , small organic molecules (glucose, galactose, cholesterol, glutamate, glycine, alanine, proline, cysteine $\big(\mathrm{Cys}\big)$ , and glutathione (GSH)), and biological fluids (serum and urine) were studied. Samples with 1000-fold dilution all exhibit negligible impact on colorimetric and fluorometric detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ except high concentrations of Cys and GSH $(200~\mu\mathbf{M})$ . The disturbance caused by high concentrations of Cys and GSH can be thoroughly eliminated through the addition of $N$ -ethylmaleimide.51 It is noteworthy that the biological samples are diluted 1000 times in actual application and the final concentrations of Cys and GSH in diluted solution are lower than $20\mu\mathrm{M},$ , indicating that they will not affect the detection of target analytes (Figure S12). These data verify that the $\mathrm{Au}_{1}/\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NR}@\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{PABA}){\mathrm{-OPD}}$ sensing platform has excellent selectivity and anti-interference ability toward $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ .
Since the $\mathbf{Au}_{1}/\mathbf{CeO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{NR}@\mathrm{Cu}\left(\mathrm{PABA}\right)-\mathrm{OPD}$ system presents the evident feasibility for colorimetric/fluorometric dual-mode detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ it may be further developed as a universal platform for the quantification of bioactive compounds that can produce $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ when catalyzed by corresponding oxidases. In this study, glucose, galactose, and cholesterol were selected as model target analytes, as $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ can be conveniently generated from glucose, galactose, and cholesterol using their respective oxidases (Glu $\begin{array}{r}{{{\mathrm{Ox}}},}\end{array}$ Gal $\begin{array}{r}{{{\mathrm{Ox}}},}\end{array}$ and Cho $\mathrm{O}\mathbf{\bar{x}}$ ) as the catalysts. The generated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ can participate in the $\mathrm{Au}_{1}/\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NR}@\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{PABA}){\mathrm{-OPD}}$ catalytic system, further result in signal intensity (absorbance and fluorescence intensity) changes, and finally achieve quantitative measurement of multiple target analytes (Scheme 1B). Significantly, glucose, galactose, and cholesterol are vital biomarkers in clinical diagnosis of manifold serious disorders;
therefore, the accurate determination of them in human body is of great practical significance for keeping good health. These biomarker-related diseases and respective normal concentrations in body fluids are specifically enumerated in Table S3.
As shown in Figure $^{8\mathrm{A,B}}$ , with a sustained increase in glucose concentration ranging from 0.05 to $400~\mu\mathrm{M},$ the absorbance at $417~\mathrm{nm}$ shows an upward trend. An ideal linear relationship in the range of 0.05 to $40~\mu\mathrm{M}$ is acquired $(y=$ $0.00135x+0.0460$ , $R^{2}=0.9972$ ) (Figure 8C), and the LOD of glucose is as low as $0.158\mu\mathrm{M}$ . Besides, a naked-eye photograph of glucose analysis system is displayed in the upper inset of Figure 8A. The color of the system solution gradually changes from colorless to yellow with the increment of glucose concentration, which agrees well with that of UV−vis absorption spectra, inferring the potential for visual detection of glucose. In the fluorometric mode, the fluorescence signals at $562~\mathrm{nm}$ intensify with increasing glucose concentration from 0.05 to $400\ \mu\mathrm{M}$ (Figure 8D,E). The linear regression equation is calculated to be $y=41.7x+1461$ with $R^{2}=0.9959$ over the linear range of $0.05{-}40~\mu\mathrm{M}$ with an LOD of $0.156\mu\mathrm{M}$ (Figure 8F). Moreover, to examine the selectivity of the analysis platform, blank and distinct interfering substances, including cholesterol, galactose, fructose, sucrose, and maltose, were separately incubated. There is no evident signal response despite the presence of $200~\mu\mathrm{M}$ interference substance; however, significant signals are captured upon introducing 40 $\mu\mathbf{M}$ glucose (Figure S13), demonstrating a high selectivity of the platform for glucose detection.
Similarly, the proposed analysis platform exhibits excellent universality in galactose and cholesterol detection by simply altering the type of oxidase. Corresponding descriptions and figures (Figure S14−S17) on sensitivity and selectivity are given in the Supporting Information. These results express the applicability of fabricated $\mathrm{Au}_{1}/\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{{NR}}(\mathrm{{\bar{\it{a}}}\mathrm{{Cu}}\mathrm{{(PA\bar{\it{B}}\mathrm{{A)}}}}}$ as a three-in-one artificial enzyme in monitoring multiple bioactive compounds.
Furthermore, it can be observed from Table S4, by comparing with the previously published literature, that the $\mathrm{Au_{1}/C e O_{2}}$ $\mathrm{NR}@\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{PABA})$ with enhanced peroxidase-like activity possesses a superior LOD for the sensing of multiple biomarkers (glucose, galactose, and cholesterol).
## CONCLUSIONS
In summary, we have developed a novel three-in-one artificial enzyme-mimetic composite by restricting $\mathrm{Au}_{1}/\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NR inside $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{PABA})}}$ . The prepared ${\mathrm{Au}}_{1}/{\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ NR@Cu(PABA) exhibits a significantly enhanced peroxidase-like activity due to the following aspects: (1) specially selected rod-shaped ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ nanocrystals with more oxygen vacancies and structure defects for catalysis; (2) synergistic effect and efficient electron transfer among Au, $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\ \mathrm{NR},$ and $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{PABA})}}$ ; and (3) the restricted structure of $\mathrm{\bfAu}_{1}/\mathrm{\bfCeO}_{2}$ NR inside $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{PABA})}}$ increasing the local concentration of active component, facilitating electron/mass transfer, and providing more active sites. Meanwhile, the obtained ${\mathrm{Au}}_{1}/{\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ $\operatorname{NR}(\varnothing\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ (PABA) with peroxidase-like activity embodies several superiorities over HRP, such as easy mass preparation, economy, high affinity toward substrates, and capability to tolerate long-term storage and extreme environments (strong acidic or basic media, high temperatures, and organic solvents). Simultaneously, colorimetric/fluorometric dual-mode determination of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is achieved through employing $\mathrm{Au}_{1}/\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{{NR}}@\mathrm{{Cu}}(\mathrm{{PABA}})$ - based sensing platform. Moreover, combined with the corresponding oxidases, $\mathrm{{Au}}_{1}/\mathrm{{Ce}}{\mathrm{{O}}}_{2}$ $\mathrm{NR}@\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{PABA})$ is used to establish a bienzyme cascade system and detect multiple biomarkers (glucose, galactose, and cholesterol) in biological fluids through monitoring $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ produced by the corresponding oxidase-catalyzed oxidation reactions. The ${\bf A u}_{1}/{\bf C e}{\bf O}_{2}$ $\mathrm{{NR}}(\varpi\mathrm{{Cu}(P A B A)}$ -based analysis platform for detection of biomarkers is characterized by the fabulous universality, which is reflected not only in facile design of other restricted structures by replacing the restriction carrier to obtain improvement of catalytic performance but also in easy detection of other biomarkers through altering the corresponding oxidase-catalyzed oxidation reaction.
The current work provides a novel functionalization and construction strategy for enzyme-like nanomaterials to improve its intrinsic catalytic activity, exhibiting a bright practical application prospect of nanozymes in the field of biosensing. However, there are still some limitations in the current study, which mainly include (1) the need to set an extra blank group and (2) the need to add the peroxidase mimic $(\mathrm{Au}_{1}/\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{{NR}}(\varpi\mathrm{{Cu}(P A B A))}$ and oxidase step by step during the detection of biomarkers. These make the detection operation relatively tedious. To date, some efforts have been devoted to simplify the detection operation steps, such as designin5g2 nanozymes with exceptional specificity toward constructing the multienzyme-restricted nanozyme for the tandem catalysis and the one-step detection of biomarkers,53 fabricating intelligently designed test kit for user-friendly and on-site analysis of biomarkers,54 and so on. With these further studies, a bright future is foreseen toward fabricating smarter artificial enzyme-based sensors for the point-of-care testing of biomarkers.
